\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around four analyses of increasing specificity: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type partial ordering (C2), the aggregate layer effect (C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 constitute our primary claims; C3 is inconclusive; C4 is exploratory but provides the model$\times$type interaction analysis.

\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, conditional OR $= 15.1$, 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity ($n = 300$, $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$\,pp, CI containing zero). A Type III sum-of-squares decomposition\footnote{Type III SS on binary outcomes approximates a linear probability model; logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:regression}) confirms concordant factor ordering (Tjur's $R^2 = 0.175$; \citealp{tjur_2009}).} partitions variance as: clause identity 22.4\% (0.4\% between types, 22.0\% within), model 1.0\%, condition 15.8\%, residual 60.8\% (conversation trajectory, user-simulator stochasticity). A permutation test ($n = 1{,}000$, shuffling within type; Appendix~\ref{app:clause_variance}) confirms the clause effect exceeds chance (null mean $= 8.3\%$, $p < 0.001$); clause-level ICC $= 0.386$ (mixed-model $= 0.189$). Individual clause wording---not type membership---drives clause-level variance; the omnibus test on ambiguous episodes nonetheless confirms significant type heterogeneity ($\chi^2 = 28.02$, $V = 0.137$; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). Among confound-controlled types, per-type effects range from $+$18.7\,pp (coreferential) to $+$36.0\,pp (authorization scope); incompleteness reaches $+$49.2\,pp but is conditional on an information-addition confound (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). A replication on two additional model families (Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}) confirms the effect across seven models ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp, both $p < 10^{-6}$); excluding incompleteness, it remains large ($\Delta = 31.0$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sans_incompleteness}). A cross-benchmark replication on SOPBench confirms directional consistency across all five models ($\Delta = +13.8$\,pp pooled; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}). The taxonomy's primary value lies not in predicting clause risk but in predicting failure mechanisms: type predicts mechanism 17$\times$ more strongly than violation rate (\S\ref{sec:discussion}), making type-level analysis (C2--C4) informative for diagnosis.

\subsection{Type Partial Ordering (C2)}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Violation rates (\%) under ambiguous policy clauses by type and model (5.4 = GPT-5.4, 4.1 = GPT-4.1, Cla.\ = Claude, Qw3 = Qwen3, DS = DeepSeek). $\Delta$pp: pooled difference (ambiguous $-$ unambiguous) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. Tier: TOST equivalence cluster at $\pm 15$\,pp---U = upper bound (separated at all margins), M = mid-tier (6/6 pairwise equivalent), L = lower (separates at $\pm 12$\,pp). All per-type $p < 10^{-5}$ (Fisher's exact).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.8pt}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrr@{\hskip 6pt}cr}
\toprule
Layer & Type & Pooled & 5.4 & 4.1 & Cla. & Qw3 & DS & $\Delta$pp\,[95\% CI] & Tier & $p$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{speccolor}{Spec.}} & Incompleteness & 53.6 & 46 & 58 & 48 & 62 & 54 & +49.2\,[42.2, 55.6] & U & $<\!10^{-37}$ \\
 & Auth.\ Scope & 41.6 & 64 & 46 & 12 & 26 & 60 & +36.0\,[29.1, 42.6] & M & $<\!10^{-22}$ \\
 & Cond.\ Prec. & 36.8 & 34 & 36 & 36 & 40 & 38 & +30.8\,[24.0, 37.4] & M & $<\!10^{-17}$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{lingcolor}{Ling.}} & Lexical & 42.8 & 42 & 34 & 32 & 52 & 54 & +35.2\,[28.0, 42.0] & M & $<\!10^{-20}$ \\
 & Scopal & 39.4 & 42 & 38 & 32 & 37 & 48 & +34.2\,[27.4, 40.7] & M & $<\!10^{-21}$ \\
 & Coreferential & 31.6 & 18 & 32 & 22 & 42 & 44 & +18.7\,[11.5, 25.7] & L & $<\!10^{-6}$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textbf{Overall}} & \textbf{41.0} & 41 & 41 & 30 & 43 & 50 & \textbf{+34.0}\,[31.3, 36.9] & --- & $<\!10^{-105}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

The six types are not interchangeable. An omnibus $\chi^2$ test on ambiguous-condition violation rates rejects the uniformity null ($\chi^2 = 28.02$, $df = 5$, $p = 3.6 \times 10^{-5}$, Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.137$). TOST equivalence testing at a $\pm 15$ pp margin reveals a graded equivalence structure among five types---authorization scope (41.6\%), lexical (42.8\%), scopal (39.4\%), and conditional precedence (36.8\%) form a mid-tier cluster, with coreferential (31.6\%) below---while incompleteness (53.6\%) stands apart as an upper bound---conditional on the confound controls in Appendix~\ref{app:confound}---whose ranking partly reflects an information-addition confound. Table~\ref{tab:main_results} and Figure~\ref{fig:forest_plot} present the full breakdown.

Sensitivity analysis across seven margins ($\pm 5$--$20$ pp; Appendix~\ref{app:tost}) confirms this structure is margin-dependent: mid-tier cohesion emerges at $\pm 12$ pp and stabilizes at $\pm 15$ pp, while the incompleteness upper-bound is robust across all margins. A bootstrap cluster analysis ($B = 10{,}000$; Appendix~\ref{app:cluster}) corroborates: incompleteness forms a singleton at $k = 2$, coreferential additionally separates at $k = 3$. Cross-model type ranking concordance is moderate ($W = 0.398$, $p = 0.072$; Appendix~\ref{app:kendall_w}), driven by incompleteness ranking highest in four of five models.

An important qualification: cross-judge-group type rankings are discordant ($\rho = 0.09$, $p = 0.87$; Appendix~\ref{app:judge_group_ranking}), driven by authorization scope ranking first in one judge group but last in the other. Excluding it, the remaining five types show strong concordance ($\rho = 0.90$, $p = 0.037$). C2 should be interpreted as establishing robust extremes---incompleteness as upper bound and coreferential as lower bound---rather than a complete linear ordering.

Incompleteness is highest in four of five models (rates $\geq$46\% in all seven; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}). Its unambiguous variant adds policy content, so the 49.2\,pp effect is an upper bound; a confound analysis confirms significance after controlling for information delta (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). Coreferential, whose variants are lexically identical, provides a confound-free lower bound at 31.6\%.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_forest_plot.pdf}
\caption{Per-type effect sizes ($\Delta$pp) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. Types form a partial ordering: incompleteness as upper bound (conditional on information-addition confound), coreferential as lower bound, and a mid-range equivalence cluster. Mid-tier ordering is margin-dependent (\S\ref{sec:results}).}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Layer Effect (C3)}

Specification-layer types trend higher than linguistic-layer types (44.0\% vs.\ 37.9\%, $p = 0.017$), but C3 is inconclusive: the effect does not survive Holm--Bonferroni correction, controlling for token delta reverses the direction, and cross-layer type overlap (conditional precedence 36.8\% vs.\ scopal 39.4\%) further undermines the distinction. Type-level distinctions (C2) hold independently of layer grouping. We accordingly treat the two-layer structure as organizational convenience rather than a theoretical claim.

\subsection{Model $\times$ Type Interaction (C4) \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:c4}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and type under ambiguous conditions ($n = 2{,}997$). Authorization scope varies 52\,pp across models ($\kappa = 0.385$); incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%). C4 interaction is authorization-scope-specific (excluding it: $p = 0.97$).}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

A model main effect is significant ($\chi^2 = 31.02$, $p = 3.0 \times 10^{-6}$, $V = 0.102$), with Claude (30.3\%) substantially less vulnerable than DeepSeek (49.7\%). Model identity explains only 1.0\% of total violation variance; the significant interaction ($F(20, 2976) = 1.97$, $p = 0.006$; $\mathrm{df} = (5{-}1) \times (6{-}1) = 20$; 2.9\% of variance; confirmed by logistic regression likelihood ratio test: $\chi^2(20) = 47.20$, $p = 5.5 \times 10^{-4}$) concentrates in specific type--model combinations.

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} highlights three patterns: (1) authorization scope is the \emph{most model-differentiating type} (52\,pp range; Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), though its low judge agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$) adds uncertainty; (2) incompleteness is a \emph{consistent vulnerability} (46--62\%); (3) conditional precedence is the most \emph{model-invariant} (34--40\%). The authorization scope ranking is stable within the GPT-5.4 judge subset (bootstrap preservation: 99.5\%), and the 34\,pp within-judge range far exceeds measurement noise; given the low cross-judge $\kappa$, we characterize this as suggestive (\S\ref{sec:limitations}). Excluding authorization scope, the residual interaction vanishes ($p = 0.97$; Appendix~\ref{app:c4_sans_auth}).

