\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A customer asks to return a customized item within the 30-day window. The agent consults the store policy, which states that ``items may be returned within 30 days.'' The word ``items'' scopes over all product categories, including custom orders that are normally final sale. The agent grants a full refund and arranges free return shipping. One word of scopal ambiguity costs the retailer \$200.

LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that govern their permitted actions. These specifications inevitably contain ambiguity: a property that has been studied for decades in requirements engineering (RE) and computational linguistics. \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish between \emph{nocuous} (i.e., harmful) ambiguity, where different stakeholders arrive at different interpretations, and \emph{innocuous} ambiguity, where shared organizational context ensures convergent interpretation. In human RE workflows, most ambiguity is innocuous because developers share domain knowledge, can consult colleagues, and iteratively clarify requirements.

LLM agents lack these disambiguation channels. They cannot consult colleagues, rarely escalate to users, and must resolve ambiguity in real time from the text alone. This raises a question that has not been empirically addressed: does policy ambiguity cause safety violations in LLM agents, and if so, does the \emph{type} of ambiguity predict the severity of the violation? Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies \citep{drip_r_2026,li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} or measures policy understanding quality \citep{policybank_2026}, but no existing study provides a causal, type-granularity mapping from ambiguity to safety violations.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{Experimental overview. We construct 300 matched clause pairs across a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy and inject them into $\tau^2$-bench policy documents. Five models interact with the modified policies across 2,997 episodes. A cross-judge evaluation pipeline classifies violation severity on a four-tier scale.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We address this gap with a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark. Using a six-type taxonomy organized into two layers---linguistic and specification, including specification-level gaps that produce equivalent behavioral effects in LLM agents (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}), we construct 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs and evaluate five architecturally diverse models across 2,997 episodes.

Our results show that policy ambiguity is consistently nocuous for LLM agents ($+34.0$ pp violation rate increase, conditional OR $= 15.1$), but risk is driven by individual clause wording (22.4\% of variance) rather than type membership (0.4\%). The taxonomy's value lies in predicting failure mechanisms: type membership predicts the dominant mechanism 17$\times$ better than it predicts violation rate ($\text{NMI} = 0.23$ vs.\ $0.01$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), enabling targeted diagnosis (\S\ref{sec:discussion}). Incompleteness is consistently the highest-risk type (46--62\% across all models); the remaining types form a partial ordering with robust extremes but margin-dependent mid-tier structure.

Our contributions are: (1)~the first matched-pair causal study of policy ambiguity's safety impact with perturbation control (\S\ref{sec:method}), observed across five primary models and confirmed in two additional replication models; (2)~a variance decomposition showing that individual clause wording (22.4\%) dominates type membership (0.4\%) and model identity (1.0\%), combined with a mutual information analysis demonstrating that type predicts failure mechanism 17$\times$ better than violation rate (\S\ref{sec:analysis}, \S\ref{sec:discussion}), repositioning the taxonomy from a risk-prediction tool to a diagnostic taxonomy for failure-mechanism prediction; and (3)~an error analysis quantifying \emph{Disambiguation Neglect} in consequential agentic settings---extending LLM ambiguity-collapse findings \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} to agentic settings---with $\leq$11\% per-model awareness (4.6\% overall), and \emph{systematic nocuity}: 41.3\% of ambiguous clauses trigger majority-convergent violations, indicating clause-level risk factors (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Ambiguity in NLP and requirements engineering.}
Ambiguity has been studied extensively in both computational linguistics and software engineering. Word sense disambiguation \citep{navigli_wsd_2009} addresses lexical ambiguity at the token level; our lexical type extends this to policy-specific polysemy. \citet{li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} propose an 11-type NLP taxonomy covering syntactic, semantic, and pragmatic ambiguity, from which we draw our linguistic-layer types. \citet{kamath_scope_2024} investigate scopal ambiguity in LLMs, finding systematic preferences for surface scope. In requirements engineering (RE), \citet{massey_ambiguity_re_2014} classify six ambiguity types in regulatory texts, and \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} introduce the distinction between nocuous ambiguity (where stakeholders diverge on interpretation) and innocuous ambiguity (where they converge). Computational detection of nocuous ambiguity has progressed from heuristic methods \citep{chantree_2006,willis_nocuous_2008} through NLP-based cross-domain detectors \citep{ferrari_ambiguity_2019,ferrari_defects_2018} to transformer-based approaches for anaphoric ambiguity \citep{ezzini_taphsir_2022,ezzini_anaphoric_2022}. \citet{zhao_nlp4re_2021} survey the broader NLP4RE landscape. Our work extends Berry and Kamsties' framework to LLM agents, where the absence of shared organizational context makes previously innocuous ambiguity nocuous.

\paragraph{Psycholinguistics of ambiguity processing.}
\citet{ferreira_good_enough_2002} show that human comprehenders build shallow, ``good-enough'' representations rather than full parses, paralleling our observation that LLM agents adopt surface interpretations. \citet{frazier_rayner_1982} demonstrate early commitment to a single parse; \citet{li_garden_path_llm_2024} provide direct evidence that LLMs exhibit analogous garden-path effects. At the pragmatic level, \citet{hu_pragmatic_2023} and \citet{ruis_goldilocks_2023} show that LLMs struggle with implicature resolution, defaulting to literal interpretations even when pragmatic inference is required. \citet{liu_ambiguity_2023} demonstrate that language models fail to represent ambiguity, collapsing multiple interpretations into a single output; \citet{gur_arieh_ambiguity_collapse_2026} taxonomize the epistemic risks of this ``ambiguity collapse''; \citet{sedova_to_know_2024} show analogous self-inconsistencies under entity-type ambiguity. These findings ground our \emph{Disambiguation Neglect} result: agents process ambiguous input without meta-cognitive awareness but, unlike humans, lack social channels to recover. \citet{disambiguation_cqa_survey_2025} survey disambiguation strategies in conversational QA; \citet{suri_structured_2025} show that structured uncertainty estimation improves coverage by 7--39\%, while \citet{sehwag_aspi_2026} demonstrate that clarification-seeking introduces prompt injection vulnerabilities---a tension for disambiguation channel design.

\paragraph{Agent safety evaluation.}
The agent safety evaluation landscape includes benchmarks for risk awareness \citep{rjudge_2024}, tool-use safety \citep{toolemu_2024}, indirect prompt injection \citep{injecagent_2024}, safe task planning \citep{yin_safeagentbench_2025}, policy-invisible violations \citep{phantompolicy_2026}, comprehensive real-world agent safety \citep{vijayvargiya_openagentsafety_2026}, trajectory-level diagnosis \citep{atbench_2026}, and deployment-condition probing \citep{riskybench_2026}. \citet{tamkin_evaluating_2023} evaluate how language models navigate policy-governed decisions, \citet{shieldagent_2025} extract formal safety rules from policy documents, and AgentAuditor \citep{agentauditor_2025} achieves human-level safety evaluation. Our work differs by focusing on the \emph{source specification} rather than the agent's execution: we measure how ambiguity in the policy itself causes safety violations, complementing work that assumes clean policies.

\paragraph{Policy specification for agents.}
DRIP-R \citep{drip_r_2026} is the closest concurrent work: it benchmarks 400 policy-ambiguous retail scenarios across five RE ambiguity types, measuring decision quality rather than safety violations. Our work differs in three respects: (1) our matched-pair design establishes causality rather than correlating ambiguity presence with outcomes; (2) we measure safety violations rather than decision quality, enabling failure mechanism analysis; (3) our two-layer taxonomy adds linguistic-layer types, and we quantify the model$\times$type interaction. We map DRIP-R's five RE ambiguity categories to our six-type taxonomy in Appendix~\ref{app:drip_mapping}. PolicyBank \citep{policybank_2026} studies iterative policy refinement, closing 82\% of the gap to a human oracle. AgentPex \citep{agentpex_2026} detects procedural violations in agentic traces, finding that 83\% of perfect-outcome traces contain violations. MANTRA \citep{mantra_2026} synthesizes SMT-validated compliance benchmarks from procedural manuals. $\tau$-bench \citep{tau_bench_2024} and its successor $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025} provide the conversational agent evaluation platforms.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around four analyses of increasing specificity: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type partial ordering (C2), the aggregate layer effect (C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 constitute our primary claims; C3 is inconclusive; C4 is exploratory but provides the model$\times$type interaction analysis.

\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, conditional OR $= 15.1$, 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity ($n = 300$, $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$\,pp, CI containing zero). A Type III sum-of-squares decomposition\footnote{Type III SS on binary outcomes approximates a linear probability model; logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:regression}) confirms concordant factor ordering (Tjur's $R^2 = 0.175$; \citealp{tjur_2009}).} partitions variance as: clause identity 22.4\% (0.4\% between types, 22.0\% within), model 1.0\%, condition 15.8\%, residual 60.8\% (conversation trajectory, user-simulator stochasticity). A permutation test ($n = 1{,}000$, shuffling within type; Appendix~\ref{app:clause_variance}) confirms the clause effect exceeds chance (null mean $= 8.3\%$, $p < 0.001$); clause-level ICC $= 0.386$ (mixed-model $= 0.189$). Individual clause wording---not type membership---drives clause-level variance; the omnibus test on ambiguous episodes nonetheless confirms significant type heterogeneity ($\chi^2 = 28.02$, $V = 0.137$; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). Among confound-controlled types, per-type effects range from $+$18.7\,pp (coreferential) to $+$36.0\,pp (authorization scope); incompleteness reaches $+$49.2\,pp but is conditional on an information-addition confound (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). A replication on two additional model families (Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}) confirms the effect across seven models ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp, both $p < 10^{-6}$); excluding incompleteness, it remains large ($\Delta = 31.0$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sans_incompleteness}). A cross-benchmark replication on SOPBench confirms directional consistency across all five models ($\Delta = +13.8$\,pp pooled; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}). The taxonomy's primary value lies not in predicting clause risk but in predicting failure mechanisms: type predicts mechanism 17$\times$ more strongly than violation rate (\S\ref{sec:discussion}), making type-level analysis (C2--C4) informative for diagnosis.

