\subsection{Layer Effect (C3)}

Specification-layer types trend higher than linguistic-layer types (44.0\% vs.\ 37.9\%, $p = 0.017$), but C3 is inconclusive: the effect does not survive Holm--Bonferroni correction and controlling for token delta reverses the direction. We treat the two-layer structure as organizational convenience rather than a theoretical claim.

\subsection{Model $\times$ Type Interaction (C4) \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:c4}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and type under ambiguous conditions ($n = 2{,}997$). Authorization scope varies 52\,pp across models ($\kappa = 0.385$); incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%). C4 interaction is authorization-scope-specific (excluding it: $p = 0.97$). Each model was evaluated by a single judge; C4 patterns should be interpreted cautiously.}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

A model main effect is significant ($\chi^2 = 31.02$, $p = 3.0 \times 10^{-6}$, $V = 0.102$), with Claude (30.3\%) substantially less vulnerable than DeepSeek (49.7\%). Model identity explains only 1.0\% of total violation variance; the significant interaction ($F(20, 2976) = 1.97$, $p = 0.006$; $\mathrm{df} = (5{-}1) \times (6{-}1) = 20$; 2.9\% of variance; confirmed by logistic regression likelihood ratio test: $\chi^2(20) = 47.20$, $p = 5.5 \times 10^{-4}$) concentrates in specific type--model combinations.

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} highlights three patterns: (1) authorization scope is the \emph{most model-differentiating type} (52\,pp range; Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), though its low judge agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$) adds uncertainty; (2) incompleteness is a \emph{consistent vulnerability} (46--62\%); (3) conditional precedence is the most \emph{model-invariant} (34--40\%). The authorization scope ranking is stable within the GPT-5.4 judge subset (bootstrap preservation: 99.5\%), and the 34\,pp within-judge range far exceeds measurement noise; given the low cross-judge $\kappa$, we characterize this as suggestive given the single-judge-per-model design (\S\ref{sec:limitations}). Excluding authorization scope, the residual interaction vanishes ($p = 0.97$; Appendix~\ref{app:c4_sans_auth}).

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Differential nocuity.}
Building on \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}'s nocuous/innocuous distinction, our results support a \emph{differential nocuity} hypothesis \citep{willis_nocuous_2008}: risk depends on type, safety training, and disambiguation channel availability. The binary effect is domain-robust (interaction $p = 0.187$) but type rankings are not ($\rho = 0.26$), suggesting nocuity is universal while its type-specific profile requires per-domain calibration. Nevertheless, 60.8\% of variance remains unexplained (conversation trajectory, user-simulator stochasticity); clause-level auditing targets the largest controllable factor (22.4\%).

\paragraph{Taxonomy as diagnostic tool.}
Type membership explains only 0.4\% of violation variance versus 22.4\% for clause wording; however, it predicts failure \emph{mechanism} 17$\times$ more strongly than violation rate (NMI ratio $\approx 17$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), validating the taxonomy as a diagnostic tool: each type maps to a dominant failure mechanism (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).

\paragraph{Prompt-based mitigation.}
A type-specific mitigation study (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) finds prompting effective for authorization scope ($-18.0$\,pp) and lexical ($-8.9$\,pp) but not incompleteness ($-3.3$\,pp, reversing across formulations), reinforcing policy-design over prompt-level fixes \citep{chantree_2006,ferrari_ambiguity_2019}.
